Package: batchit
Title: Run Long R Work Across Processes or a Scheduler
Version: 26.8.28
Authors@R:
    c(
      person(
        given = c("Richard", "Aubrey"),
        family = "White",
        email = "hello@rwhite.no",
        role = c("aut", "cre", "cph"),
        comment = c(ORCID = "0000-0002-6747-1726")
      )
    )
Description: Runs one R function once per item in a list of inputs, each call in
    its own worker process. Return values come back as a list, or are discarded
    when the function is called for a side effect. A third mode writes the
    function's declared output files for it. Naming the function by package and
    symbol, rather than passing it by value, lets a worker hash what it loaded.
    The worker then refuses code that differs from what was dispatched. batchit
    checks every item's arguments against the function's formals, in both the
    calling session and the worker. An item that completes carries its warnings
    back, and the calling session re-raises them labelled with that item. An item
    that fails stops the call rather than returning a partial result. batchit
    stages output files beside their destinations and renames them into place, so
    a failed item leaves no half-written file. Two transports run the work in this
    session's own child processes. One starts a fresh process per item via
    'processx', where process exit is what reclaims memory. The other is a lazy
    producer with bounded backpressure over 'mirai' daemons, for items generated
    on demand. Work too long for one session goes to a scheduler instead. Three
    functions describe a chain of 'Slurm' jobs, write the job scripts plus a
    driver that submits them, and run that driver. The call that writes the
    chain submits nothing. Submission is a separate call, so a person can read
    the driver first.
Depends: R (>= 4.1.0)
